WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.111
[soft ambient music]

00:00:03.111 --> 00:00:07.000
Chess never needed sight.

00:00:07.000 --> 00:00:10.202
Forty-three million people are blind.

00:00:10.202 --> 00:00:13.317
Two hundred ninety-five million live

00:00:13.317 --> 00:00:16.000
with serious visual impairment.

00:00:16.000 --> 00:00:18.519
Chess is one of the very few games

00:00:18.519 --> 00:00:21.185
they play on completely equal terms.

00:00:21.185 --> 00:00:24.000
The game itself needs no sight at all.

00:00:24.000 --> 00:00:25.682
Then you open a chess app.

00:00:25.682 --> 00:00:28.271
It's built around a drag-and-drop board,

00:00:28.271 --> 00:00:30.082
so a screen reader reads out

00:00:30.082 --> 00:00:31.571
sixty-four empty cells.

00:00:31.571 --> 00:00:33.900
The hard part becomes the interface,

00:00:33.900 --> 00:00:35.000
not the opponent.

00:00:35.000 --> 00:00:38.136
Every workaround costs you something.

00:00:38.136 --> 00:00:41.102
A helper who has to be in the room.

00:00:41.102 --> 00:00:43.814
A braille board that can't reach

00:00:43.814 --> 00:00:45.000
anyone online.

00:00:45.000 --> 00:00:48.500
[music swells]

00:00:48.500 --> 00:00:52.000
VoiceChessmate

00:00:52.000 --> 00:00:56.359
You hold one key and say the move.

00:00:56.359 --> 00:01:00.462
It plays, answers, and explains,

00:01:00.462 --> 00:01:02.000
out loud.

00:01:02.000 --> 00:01:04.595
The moves are spoken in the IBCA

00:01:04.595 --> 00:01:06.216
tournament alphabet.

00:01:06.216 --> 00:01:08.324
Anna, Bella, Cesar, David,

00:01:08.324 --> 00:01:10.514
Eva, Felix, Gustav, Hector.

00:01:10.514 --> 00:01:13.027
It's the notation blind players

00:01:13.027 --> 00:01:14.000
already use.

00:01:14.000 --> 00:01:16.368
B, D, E and G sound almost identical

00:01:16.368 --> 00:01:17.568
over a microphone.

00:01:17.553 --> 00:01:19.592
That's the alphabet chess uses.

00:01:19.592 --> 00:01:22.289
A misheard letter isn't an error message.

00:01:22.289 --> 00:01:24.000
It's a legal, losing move.

00:01:24.000 --> 00:01:26.785
You can also just ask.

00:01:26.785 --> 00:01:30.329
Anything about the position,

00:01:30.329 --> 00:01:34.000
answered from the real board.

00:01:34.000 --> 00:01:37.208
And when you've done something stupid,

00:01:37.208 --> 00:01:39.487
you can ask about that too.

00:01:39.487 --> 00:01:43.286
That answer isn't a language model's opinion.

00:01:43.286 --> 00:01:47.000
It's the engine's real evaluation, in pawns.

00:01:47.000 --> 00:01:49.319
Underneath, audio streams as PCM sixteen

00:01:49.319 --> 00:01:51.290
over one WebSocket to AssemblyAI's

00:01:51.290 --> 00:01:52.490
Voice Agent API.

00:01:52.217 --> 00:01:54.014
Speech to text, turn detection,

00:01:54.014 --> 00:01:56.507
the model and the voice, on one connection.

00:01:56.507 --> 00:01:57.725
Eighteen typed tools,

00:01:57.725 --> 00:01:59.000
against a real engine.

00:01:59.000 --> 00:02:01.811
And when it isn't sure, it doesn't guess.

00:02:01.811 --> 00:02:04.349
It reads back the real legal options.

00:02:04.349 --> 00:02:07.571
A sighted player undoes a wrong move instantly.

00:02:07.571 --> 00:02:09.766
A blind player might not find it

00:02:09.766 --> 00:02:11.000
for several moves.

00:02:11.000 --> 00:02:12.862
Which isn't only for people

00:02:12.862 --> 00:02:14.517
who can't see the board.

00:02:14.517 --> 00:02:16.586
Blindfold play is a discipline

00:02:16.586 --> 00:02:17.897
club players train.

00:02:17.897 --> 00:02:21.000
Hands-free means you can play while you walk.

00:02:21.000 --> 00:02:24.944
It's live, open source, and covered

00:02:24.944 --> 00:02:29.000
by six hundred and twenty-six tests.

00:02:29.000 --> 00:02:33.167
Chess never needed sight.

00:02:33.167 --> 00:02:38.000
Its software just assumed it.

00:02:38.000 --> 00:02:39.592
[music fades]

00:02:39.592 --> 00:02:44.000
voicechessmate - built on AssemblyAI
